Non-brand paid search spend in Q3 2025 was about $1,308,447. That is the sum of the `FH_Search_NonBrand` campaign's cost in the Google Ads weekly data. It covers the 13 weeks whose Monday falls in Q3, from 2025-07-07 through 2025-09-29.

I treated "non-brand paid search" as that one campaign. The only other paid search campaign is `FH_Search_Brand`, and the YouTube campaign is video, not search. Only Google Ads has search campaigns in the data, so Bing and other search engines aren't included.

{"answer": 1308446.91, "refused": false}

Google Ads had the highest spend in 2023, at about $6.58M. The next highest was Meta at about $1.91M. LinkedIn ($1.85M), CTV ($1.58M) and Display ($1.23M) were lower still.

I counted weeks by their Monday, as the house rules say. Partner fees ($0.73M) and webinar costs ($0.71M) were both well below Google, so whether they count as paid channels doesn't change the result.

{"answer": "Google", "refused": false}

Texas Connected TV spend in 2024 was **$162,081.69**. That is the sum of `spend` in `ctv_weekly` for `US_TX` (US Texas) over the 22 weeks with a Monday in 2024, from 2024-01-01 to 2024-12-30.

The table has rows for only 22 of the 52 weeks in 2024. Every region has similar gaps across the three years, so I treated the missing weeks as periods when no CTV ran. If those weeks are actually missing data, the true total would be higher.

{"answer": 162081.69, "refused": false}

Meta delivered 50,843,074 impressions in the first half of 2025. That covers the 26 weeks whose Monday falls between 2025-01-06 and 2025-06-30. The week starting 2024-12-30 is left out because its Monday is in 2024, and the week starting 2025-06-30 is included.

The Meta table had no duplicate week-and-region rows or null impressions.

{"answer": 50843074, "refused": false}

LinkedIn's 2024 click-through rate was about 0.60%. That is 179,615 clicks on 29,819,086 impressions, or 0.6023%.

The table has no rows for the four weeks of 2024-09-30, 10-07, 10-14 and 10-21, so the figure covers 49 of the 53 weeks that start in 2024. The rate has stayed within 0.600–0.603% every year, so the gap probably doesn't change it much. I haven't confirmed that, though.

{"answer": 0.60, "refused": false}

Non-brand paid search cost per click in Q2 2024 was about $7.07. That is $1,095,806.03 in spend divided by 154,930 clicks.

I used the `FH_Search_NonBrand` campaign in the Google Ads weekly data. Q2 covers the 13 weeks starting 2024-04-01 through 2024-06-24, assigned by each week's Monday. The data has one row per week and region, with no duplicates or missing values.

{"answer": 7.07, "refused": false}

YouTube's CPM in 2023 was about $10.31. That's $584,214.82 of spend over 56,687,851 impressions, times 1,000.

I treated YouTube as the Google Ads `VIDEO` campaign type, which holds only the `FH_YouTube_Prospecting` campaign. I assigned weeks to 2023 by their Monday start date, per the house rules.

{"answer": 10.31, "refused": false}

Meta reported 56,233 conversions in 2025. That figure is the sum of the `leads` column in `ads_meta_weekly`, which is the only conversion-type metric Meta's export has. I counted a week in 2025 if its Monday falls in 2025, so the first week is 2025-01-06 and the last is 2025-12-22. The data had no duplicate rows or nulls for that period.

Treating "conversions" as Meta's leads is my assumption, since the export has no column labeled conversions.

{"answer": 56233, "refused": false}

There were 42,439 organic search leads in 2024. I counted CRM leads created from 2024-01-01 to 2024-12-31 where `utm_medium` is "organic". That is 37,372 from Google and 5,067 from Bing. Each lead ID appears once, so there are no duplicates in the count.

I counted both search engines as organic search. If you only want Google, the figure is 37,372.

{"answer": 42439, "refused": false}

There were 10,569 closed-won deals in 2025. That counts opportunities with stage "Closed Won" and a close date from 2025-01-01 through 2025-12-31. Every opportunity ID is unique, so no deal is counted twice.

I used the calendar close date rather than the Monday-based week rule, because the opportunities table records dates, not weeks.

{"answer": 10569, "refused": false}

Closed-won revenue in Q4 2024 was $7,674,450. That is the sum of `amount` on `crm_opportunities` rows with stage "Closed Won" and a `close_date` from 2024-10-01 to 2024-12-31. It covers 2,683 opportunities, and I found no duplicate opportunity IDs.

I used the close date as the date of the revenue. The data has no separate booking or invoice date.

{"answer": 7674450, "refused": false}

The 2024 win rate was 32.83% of closed deals. That is 11,212 Closed Won out of 34,151 closed (11,212 won plus 22,939 lost).

I used close date to assign deals to 2024. Open opportunities have no close date, so they're excluded. Opportunity IDs are unique, so nothing is double-counted.

{"answer": 32.83, "refused": false}

The 2024 email open rate was about 29.17%. That is 1,574,578 unique opens divided by 5,397,978 emails sent.

I counted weeks by their Monday, so the 53 weeks from 2024-01-01 to 2024-12-30 are all included. They cover all 20 regions, and no values were missing.

